6. LLM vs NLP

This is an important distinction.

What is NLP?

NLP = Natural Language Processing

NLP is a field of Artificial Intelligence and Computer Science focused on processing and working with human language.

Examples:

NLP
 │
 ├── Translation
 ├── Sentiment Analysis
 ├── Text Classification
 ├── Summarization
 ├── Question Answering
 └── Named Entity Recognition
What is an LLM?

An LLM is a type of language model that can perform many language-related tasks.

Simple Difference
NLP	LLM
A field of study	A type of model
Focuses on human language	Focuses on learning language patterns
Contains many techniques	Uses neural networks
Can use task-specific models	Can perform many tasks using one model
Remember

NLP is the field. LLM is a type of model.

It is not completely correct to say:

"NLP is only designed for specific tasks."

Instead:

Traditional NLP applications often used task-specific approaches, while modern LLMs can handle many NLP tasks using one general-purpose model.

7. What makes LLMs so powerful?

There is no single secret.

Several important technologies and ideas work together.

7.1 Massive Amounts of Data

LLMs can be trained on very large datasets containing text.

Examples may include:

Books
Articles
Web Documents
Code
Educational Content
Other Text Data

Large and diverse datasets help models learn many language patterns.

7.2 Large Number of Parameters

LLMs can have billions or more learned parameters.

More parameters can give a model greater capacity to represent complex patterns.

But:

More parameters does not automatically mean better performance.

7.3 Deep Neural Networks

LLMs are based on neural networks with many computational layers.

Input
  ↓
Layer 1
  ↓
Layer 2
  ↓
Layer 3
  ↓
Layer 4
  ↓
Output

More layers allow the model to learn increasingly complex representations.

7.4 Transformer Architecture

The Transformer architecture is one of the biggest breakthroughs behind modern LLMs.

Transformers use mechanisms such as attention to model relationships between tokens.

7.5 Self-Supervised Learning

LLMs can learn from raw text without manually labeling every piece of data.

Example:

The capital of France is ______

The model learns to predict:

Paris

This allows huge amounts of text to be used for training.

7.6 Scaling

Modern AI systems can improve by scaling combinations of:

Data
+
Model Size
+
Compute
+
Better Training

This is one reason modern LLMs became so capable.

