16. RAG — Retrieval-Augmented Generation

RAG is an important concept for LLM applications.

RAG = Retrieval-Augmented Generation

Instead of depending only on the information learned during training, an application retrieves relevant information and gives it to the LLM as context.

Without RAG
User Question
      ↓
LLM
      ↓
Answer
With RAG
User Question
      ↓
Search Knowledge Base
      ↓
Relevant Information
      ↓
LLM
      ↓
Answer
Example

Suppose a company has:

Insurance Documents
Company Policies
FAQs
Product Information

RAG can retrieve relevant information before asking the LLM to generate an answer.

17. Fine-Tuning

Fine-tuning means taking an existing model and training it further on specific data or desired behavior.

Base LLM
   ↓
Specific Dataset
   ↓
Fine-Tuning
   ↓
Specialized Model

Example:

General LLM
     ↓
Insurance-specific training
     ↓
Insurance-focused model
RAG vs Fine-Tuning
RAG	Fine-Tuning
Retrieves information at runtime	Changes model through additional training
Useful for external/current/private knowledge	Useful for behavior/style/domain adaptation
Doesn't normally change base model parameters	Updates model parameters in standard fine-tuning
Easy to update documents	Updating behavior requires additional training
